RevivedHack is my extensively rewritten adaptation of Don G.
Kneller's PC Hack 3.61 to modern operating systems. It
currently runs on Windows, DOS, and Linux. Windows and Linux
versions are available from my hope page:

http://www.freewebs.com/drussell/RevivedHack.htm

(My email address is on the News page)

For those unfamiliar with this game:

Back in 1987 Mike Stephenson merged several different
versions of Hack (which started as a clone of Rogue) into
one game, which was called NetHack 1.4. Many relics of Hack
can still be found in NetHack (all of the monsters, for
example), but Hack was a complete and enjoyable Roguelike in
its own right.

The existing DOS versions of Hack dating from the mid-1980's
are quickly becoming difficult to compile due to the age of
the development tools required, and the state of the code
itself. The code was written for a 16-bit compiler, and was
intended to run on a PC with one or two floppy disk drives,
possibly no hard drive, and a few hundred kilobyes of memory.
The graphics relied on an ANSI.SYS driver that was loaded at
startup, and a TERMCAP file was required to tell the program
how to control the cursor on the screen.

For my new version, I rewrote many sections of the program,
making the game more portable and easily compiled with
recent development tools without changing any of the
gameplay elements. I improved the graphics by adding color,
and by using DOS OEM characters for the walls, doors, etc.
on the Windows/DOS platform. I also fixed some gameplay bugs
that were discovered many years ago, but were never fixed.
Finally, save and bones files are 100% compatible between
ports with the same version number.

I have tested the game thoroughly, winning it once recently,
and have included an updated guide originally written by Don
Kneller.

Like my previous Rogue adaptations, I hope to draw new
players to this largely abandoned yet very worthy Roguelike
game.



